<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The redroid arm64 GApps image declares only:

      /vendor/etc/permissions/android.hardware.touchscreen.xml

  and ships no android.hardware.touchscreen.multitouch*.xml at all, even though
  its own /vendor/etc/permissions/handheld_core_hardware.xml says:

      "devices that support multitouch must include the most appropriate one of
       these files: android.hardware.touchscreen.multitouch.xml /
       .multitouch.distinct.xml / .multitouch.jazzhand.xml
       ONLY ONE of the above should be included."

  eFootball (jp.konami.pesam 11.0.1) declares:

      <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.touchscreen.multitouch"/>

  android:required is omitted, which the Android spec defines as required=true.
  With the feature absent from `pm list features`, Google Play's device
  compatibility check refuses the app with "This app isn't compatible with your
  device" while unrelated apps (CPU-Z) install normally.

  Adding the missing declaration is a capability declaration, not an identity
  change: redroid's input pipeline is a stock Android InputReader attached to a
  virtual touchscreen and does dispatch multi-pointer MotionEvents.

  Android's own spec file for exactly this case is the basic (non-independent)
  variant, so that is what is installed here.
-->
<permissions>
    <feature name="android.hardware.touchscreen.multitouch" />
</permissions>